  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 202 AM MDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nMostly quiet weather expected to start the long term forecast as \nupper-level flow turns west-southwesterly and weakens for Thursday. \nThe next upper-level trough will start to become more amplified off \nthe coast of the Pacific Northwest throughout the day Thursday, \nwhile a shortwave develops off the coast of central California. \nExpecting a nice day on Thursday with highs in the upper-50s to \nupper-60s across the region with sunny skies turning partly cloudy \ninto the overnight hours. The aforementioned upper-level shortwave \nwill move quickly to the northeast Thursday into Friday as it moves \ninland with long range models currently suggesting it will impact \nthe region late Friday evening into the overnight hours. However, \nthere is some model disagreement on how quick this shortwave ejects \nnorthward. The GFS suggests the shortwave moving through between 06 \nand 18Z on Saturday, while the ECMWF suggest delayed impact between \n18 Saturday and 06Z Sunday. Precipitation chances are anticipated \nwith the passage of the shortwave, with isolated showers possible \nacross the Panhandle as early as 18Z Friday, but a delayed shortwave \nwould delay the increasing precipitation chances across the region. \nClouds will accompany this shortwave, with mostly cloudy skies \nexpected throughout the day Friday as the shortwave approaches the \nregion. Friday will be mild with temperatures in the low- to mid-60s \neverywhere.\n\nWhile the longer range models had some significant timing \ndifferences with the upper-level shortwave moving through, the GFS \nand ECMWF are in good agreement with the progression of the incoming \nupper-level trough. This trough is currently progged to move inland \nSaturday evening, strengthening as it does so. The CWA is progged to \nbe caught between the Sub-Tropical jet across the southern half of \nthe CONUS and the incoming Polar Jet across the northern half of the \nCWA. As a result, a brief period of zonal flow is anticipated early \nSunday morning before upper-level winds turn southwesterly with the \napproaching system. Monday onwards, long range models start to \ndiverge once again with the GFS suggesting the upper-level low \ndiving southward into northern New Mexico. The ECMWF on the other \nhand broadens the low and advects it directly overhead, before \nelongating the trough southward Tuesday morning. Both evolutions \nwould bring widespread precipitation into the region Monday and \nTuesday, but the ECMWF evolution would keep precipitation around \nlonger, likely leading to higher accumulation values. Another \nglaring difference between the GFS and the ECMWF is the 700mb \ntemperatures associated with this system. The ECMWF is much warmer \nthan the GFS, keeping 700mb temperatures around -4 to -2C range, \nwith a brief period of -5C. The GFS, however, suggests a cold system \nwith a longer duration of -5C temperatures across southern portions \nof the region. Both long range models suggest that some snow would \nbe likely, especially in the overnight hours, with this system, but \nthe GFS evolution would likely produce more snow, given the longer \nduration of colder temperatures. This evolution will continue to be \nmonitored over the upcoming model runs, to start getting a better \nidea of storm evolution and precipitation totals. Despite this, \ncooler temperatures are expected to return for much of next\nweek.",
